She wasn't Greek, IIRC, but appeared in the story of Jason and the
Golden Fleece.

Lets see what my nifty thick book of mythology[0] says.

	Media was the daughter of the Colchian king Æetes.  Colchis was
	on the eastern shore of the Black Sea, which would put it
	somewhere in the Ukraine, Russia, or the Caucasian States.
	
	Jason, who was from Thessaly and thus presumably Greek, wanted
	the Fleece in order to gain the crown to a local kingdom from
	his uncle.
	
	Once Jason (and the rest of his sailing crew) were in Colchis,
	Jason promised marriage to Media.  In Bulfinch's words, Medea
	was "a potent sorceress" and was able to help Jason with most of
	the challenges that Æetes imposed upon Jason[1].  Finally (again
	with Medea's help), Jason was able to grab the golden fleece
	from a sleeping dragon and quickly leave town[2].
	
	Once back in his Thessalian kingdom, Medea murdered Jason's
	uncle (which would presumably made Jason king).  In order to
	repay her, Jason divorced her and married a Greek.  In revenge,
	Medea killed her own children, murdered Jason's new bride, burnt
	down the palace, and fled to Athens, where she married King
	Ægeus.
	
	In Athens, Medea tried to kill her stepchild, but failed.  Once
	more she fled, this time to Asia, where (according to Bulfinch)
	the country of Media received its name from her.

I remember reading a book years ago called _The_Dawn_Palace_ which give
a different version of the story.  Rather good, if I recall correctly.

